WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.529 --> 00:00:03.510
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.460
- This is the only place in San Francisco

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.650
you can see bison.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.973
This is the best spot to
view the Golden Gate Bridge.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.823
This is one of the oldest
gay bars in the city, cheers.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:22.050
But it all starts here,
at the concierge desk

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.066
of the Fairmont, San Francisco.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.649
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.730
I'm Tom Wolfe, and I'm
America's first concierge.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.300
I was very lucky because
my parents loved to go out,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:43.300
so I was constantly seeing
the world of hospitality,

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.600
the world of new restaurants,
the world of sophistication,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.660
and that's kind of where
the seed was planted,

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:50.960
shall we say.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.770
My first hotel job was
working in Washington D.C.,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.690
and I then went to London,
and the general manager

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.091
asked me, "Have you got a morning suit?"

00:01:00.091 --> 00:01:02.020
Luckily, I knew what a morning suit was,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.550
and I said, “Of course, Mr. Schwinter, I do.”

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.510
My next port of call would be Paris.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.280
I managed to get a job
at a very luxurious hotel

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.060
right on the Champs-Elysées,
and I learned many lessons.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.166
I made my way back to the United States,

00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:18.193
where I found myself in San Francisco.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.193
I started as the concierge
at the Fairmont in 1974.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.870
The Fairmont Hotel occupies
an entire square block.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.920
The main building opened in
1907, one year to the day

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.850
after the great fire.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.663
If these walls could whisper,
what stories they would tell.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.010
A secret door, that could
be used for a discreet exit.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:48.123
The Tonga Room was once
The Fairmont swimming pool.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.960
If you can think of somebody
important, they were here.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.960
Whether they were from
the world of Hollywood,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.530
or the world of politics.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.300
Pretty much every president
has stayed here at one time

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.133
or another.

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:04.980
And we had the first person
to break the color barrier

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.690
in the Venetian Room, where
an entertainer of color

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.970
would be able to stay at the same place

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.490
where they performed.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:15.960
The Fairmont philosophy has always been,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.033
the best kind of guest you can
hope for is a repeat guest.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.630
(light music)

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.300
When I'm out and about
walking to this very day,

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.250
I'm still exploring.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.810
And I find these little streets
that I haven't been down,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.550
or I find a shop that I've not been in,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.013
and I take the time to go in,

00:02:37.013 --> 00:02:40.120
say hello and introduce myself.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.000
It's a constant learning experience.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.490
And the minute you stop
learning, well, you might as well

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:45.323
stop working.

00:02:46.285 --> 00:02:48.868
(light music)

